<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 322 0 R
/T ([5] Ntirogiannis, Konstantinos, Basilis Gatos, and Ioannis Pratikakis, "A Performance Evaluation Methodology for Historical Document Image Binarization." : 1-1, 2017 )
/E ([5] Ntirogiannis, Konstantinos, Basilis Gatos, and Ioannis Pratikakis, "A Performance Evaluation Methodology for Historical Document Image Binarization." : 1-1, 2017 )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
606 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 322 0 R
/E (Document image binarization is of great importance in the document image analysis and recognition pipeline since it affects further stages of the recognition process. The evaluation of a binarization method aids in studying its algorithmic behavior, as well as verifying its effectiveness, by providing qualitative and quantitative indication of its performance. This paper addresses a pixel-based binarization evaluation methodology for historical handwritten/machine-printed document images. In the proposed evaluation scheme, the recall and precision evaluation measures are properly modified using a weighting scheme that diminishes any potential evaluation bias. Additional performance metrics of the proposed evaluation scheme consist of the percentage rates of broken and missed text, false alarms, background noise, character enlargement, and merging. Several experiments conducted in comparison with other pixel-based evaluation measures demonstrate the validity of the proposed evaluation scheme. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
607 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 323 0 R
/E (15 )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
608 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 323 0 R
/T (3.SYSTEM DESIGN )
/E (3.SYSTEM DESIGN )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
609 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 323 0 R
/E (The verification of certificates plays a vital role in ensuring the authenticity and credibility of academic, professional, and official credentials. Traditional certificate validation methods rely on manual verification, where institutions, employers, or regulatory authorities cross-check documents against records or contact the issuing organization for authentication. However, this approach is time-consuming, inefficient, and prone to human error, making it difficult to detect forged or tampered certificates. Additionally, the increasing prevalence of fraudulent credentials poses a serious threat to educational institutions, businesses, and government agencies, emphasizing the need for a more secure and automated solution. To overcome these limitations, AI-based certificate validation systems have emerged as a highly effective alternative, utilizing advanced technologies such as Optical Character Recognition \(OCR\), Natural Language Processing \(NLP\), and machine learning algorithms to streamline the verification process. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
610 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 323 0 R
/E (The AI-powered certificate validation system is designed to extract and analyze information from digital or scanned certificates, identifying inconsistencies and ensuring accuracy through automated checks. OCR technology enables the extraction of textual and graphical data, allowing the system to recognize and process information efficiently. NLP techniques help interpret and analyze certificate content, while machine learning models detect anomalies, such as mismatched fonts, incorrect formatting, or altered signatures, which are often difficult to identify through manual inspection. Additionally, Ai technology can be integrated to provide a decentralized and tamper-proof ledger for storing verified certificates, ensuring their authenticity and preventing unauthorized modifications. QR codes and digital signatures further enhance security, allowing quick and easy verification through a simple scan or online lookup. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
611 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 323 0 R
/E (The system architecture consists of multiple key components, including a user-friendly web or mobile interface where users can upload certificates for validation, a data processing module for extracting and analyzing information, an AI engine for detecting fraud, and a secure database or Ai ledger for storing validated records. The implementation process begins with data collection, where authentic certificates from various institutions are gathered to train the AI model. The system is then developed and tested using machine learning algorithms to enhance accuracy in fraud detection. Once fully optimized, the AI-powered validation platform can be deployed across multiple sectors, including education, corporate hiring platforms, and government agencies, enabling seamless and real-time certificate authentication. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
612 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 323 0 R
/E (By automating the verification process, AI-driven systems offer numerous advantages, including increased accuracy, faster processing, and enhanced security. Unlike traditional manual methods, which are labor-intensive and error-prone, AI-based validation ensures that certificates are verified with high precision and minimal human )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
613 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
